# Recent advances in metal-organic frameworks for X-ray detection

1College of Materials Science and Engineering, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou 350116, China; 2College of Chemistry, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou 350116, China; 3College of Materials Science and Engineering, Fujian University of Technology, Fuzhou 350118, Chin
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of fascinating supramolecular crystalline materials that have been widely developed for catalysis, gas storage, illumination, drug delivery/cytoprotection, and so on. Recently, MOFs have been found to have potential applications in X-ray detection due to their high sensitivity, fast response time, high absorption coefficient, and radiation stability. In this review, we present an overview on the fundamental mechanism of using MOFs for X-ray scintillation. We further discuss the recent developments in X-ray detection based on indirect X-ray scintillation and direct X-ray conversion. Finally, we provide a summary and a perspective on the future of this promising research field.
metal-organic frameworks, scintillation, X-ray detection, semiconducting
Citation: Chen H, Chen J, Li M, You M, Chen Q, Lin M, Yang H. Recent advances in metal-organic frameworks for X-ray detection. SciChinaChem, 2022, 65: 2338–2350, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-022-1334-0
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of supramolecular crystalline materials constructed from metal-containing nodes (metal cations or metal clusters) and organic ligands by well-defined coordination bonds [1,2]. Since the 1990s, thousands of MOFs have been synthesized by using different species of metal ions (or clusters) and organic ligands [3,4]. Over the past decades, MOFs have been extensively used in gas storage, catalysis, and adsorptive separation due to their ultrahigh porosity $(>90\%$ free volume), large internal surface area, fully exposed active sites, and tuneable pores [3,5–9]. With the development of MOFs, the properties of photoluminescence and semiconductor performance with photoelectric conversion have attracted tremendous researchers to extend their applications into chemical sensors, electrical devices, and biomedicines
[1,10–17].
Recently, MOFs have been found exhibiting efficient luminescence or electrical signal output under X-ray irradiation by using high atomic number $(Z)$ heavy metal clusters as the radiation absorption medium and organic linkers as the functional component. In 2014, Wang et al. [18] reported Xray-excited luminescent MOFs that were constructed from $\mathrm{M}_{6}(\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-O}})_{4}(\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}})_{4}$ (carboxylate) $\mid_{12}$ $\mathbf{\mathrm{M}}=\mathbf{\mathrm{Hf}}$ or $Z\mathrm{r}$ ) secondary building units (SBUs) and anthracene-based dicarboxylate bridging ligands. The materials showed high X-ray-to-photon conversion efficiency and fast decay time of $^{<7}$ ns. In 2019, Wang et al. [19] developed a direct $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray detector based on a semiconducting MOF as an active layer, and the device showed a high $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray sensitivity as compared to the commercial amorphous selenium (α-Se) X-ray detector. Therefore, MOFs show great potential to realize high resolution X-ray imaging and high sensitivity X-ray detection.
In the past decade, many reviews have been presented to discuss the development of luminescent [1,10,20,21] and semiconducting MOFs [22]. However, there are few reviews on the study of X-ray detection based on MOFs. In this review, we summarize the development of MOFs with X-ray luminescence and direct X-ray photoelectric conversion for X-ray detection. We first introduce the fundamental mechanisms of using MOFs for X-ray scintillation and direct Xray detection. Next, we discuss the technological development of indirect X-ray scintillation and direct X-ray detection using MOFs as energy converters. Finally, we conclude with a summary and a perspective on the future of this research field.
## 2 The basics for X-ray detection
X-ray detectors are mainly divided into indirect-type and direct-type X-ray detectors [23]. As shown in Figure 1a, the structure of an indirect X-ray detector generally contains two components: (1) scintillator, which is used to convert X-ray photons to ultra-violate or visible (UV/Vis) light; (2) phototransistor, which converts UV/Vis into an electrical signal [24]. Owing to the advantages of high operation stability, low fabrication cost, and high temporal resolution, it has been widely used in various computerized tomography (CT) imaging equipment [25,26]. While for a direct X-ray detector, the electron-hole pairs are directly generated by absorbing incident X-ray photons, then moving opposite to the electrodes under an applied bias to produce an electrical signal, as indicated in Figure 1b. Because of its simple device structure, it takes the advantages of simple system complexity, simple device fabrication, and high spatial solution [27].
## 2.1 Basic scintillation mechanism
Energy conversion of X-rays in scintillators generally involves three physical processes: (1) photoelectric effect, which is the process of an inner shell electron excited and ejected out of the atom by absorbing an incident X-ray photon [28]; (2) Compton scattering, where the incident Xray photons inelastically interact with bound electrons, and the energy of X-ray photons would partly transfer to the electrons, with the scattering angle as the main factor for the energy loss of X-ray photons [29]; (3) pair production, where the X-ray photons may convert into an electron-positron pair when the energy is higher than $1.02\mathrm{MeV}$ [28,30]. The photoelectric effect mainly occurs at the incident X-ray photon energy below hundreds of $\mathrm{keV}$ and Compton scattering happens at energies between a few hundred $\mathrm{keV}$ to ${<}1\mathrm{MeV}$ [27,30]. At present, the effect of $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -rays on MOFs are mainly concerned with the photoelectric effect, and thus we would review the interaction between X-ray and MOFs with the energy below hundreds of $\displaystyle\mathrm{keV.}$
## 2.1.1 Scintillation process in MOFs
Similar to other types of scintillators, MOFs can be excited by X-rays to produce light emission [18,31,32], which consists of three main stages: conversion, transport, and luminescence (Figure 2a). The conversion stage would involve a photoelectric effect when the energy of incident X-ray at $100\mathrm{eV}{<}h\nu{<}100\mathrm{keV}.$ It can generate highly energetic electrons and deep holes after the MOFs absorb the incident Xray photons. The $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray energy absorption can be described by Eq. (1):
$$
{\frac{I_{x}}{I_{i x}}}=\mathbf{e}^{-{\mu_{a}}d}
$$
where $I_{i x}$ is the intensity of incident X-ray, $I_{x}$ is the intensities of the transmitted $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray, $\mu_{a}$ is the linear absorption coefficient, and $d$ is the thickness of the materials [28]. Meanwhile, the kinetic energy of the hot electrons is $E_{x}–E_{B}$ , where $E_{x}$ is X-ray energy, and $E_{B}$ is the binding energy of the MOFs [29]. By Auger processes and electron scattering, lots of secondary electrons are produced in the crystal lattice with a decreasing of kinetic energy. Then, the secondary electrons would further reduce the kinetic energy by interacting with the phonons, and thus those carriers gradually accumulate at the valence band (holes) and conduction band (elections).
At the transport stage, these electrons and holes would migrate to the luminescence centers in a duration of 10−12– ${{10}^{-8}}\mathrm{s}$ . For the luminescence center of MOFs, it can be the luminescent organic bridging ligands (i.e., anthracene core derivatives), heavy metal clusters (i.e., $\mathrm{UO}_{2}^{2+}$ or lanthanide), or the emission guest in the framework [18,33,34]. The free carriers can be captured by defects and result in nonradiative losses. Therefore, the crystal growth condition of MOFs should be well optimized to reduce defect density as much as possible [30]. Finally, the radiative recombination of the excitons or electron-hole pairs has happened and the material emits ultraviolet or visible light at the luminescence stage.
During this process, various possibilities contribute to the luminescence of MOFs [20,35,36], including metal-to-ligand charge transfer (MLCT), ligand-to-ligand charge transfer (LLCT), ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT), and metalto-metal charge transfer (MMCT), as well as processes that involve guest molecules in the MOF pores, such as guestcentered emission and guest-sensitization, as seen in Figure 2b [37].
## 2.1.2 Key scintillator performance metrics
To evaluate the performance of a scintillator, the following key metrics should be considered.
Absorption coefficient $(\mu_{a})$ . It determines the X-ray absorption efficiency of a scintillator at the conversion stage. For a scintillator with a given thickness, $\mu_{a}$ can be described as Eq. (2) [30]:
$$
\mu_{{\mathrm a}}={\frac{\rho Z^{4}}{A_{m}E^{3}}}
$$
where $\rho,Z,E$ , and $A_{m}$ are the density, atomic number, radiation energy, and atomic mass, respectively.
Light yield $(L Y)$ . It is defined as the production of the photons emitted by unit incident energy, which mainly depends on the efficiency of electron-hole pairs migrating to luminescence center $(S_{e-h})$ , the luminescence quantum yield $(\phi)$ , and energy bandgap $(E_{g})$ of the scintillator. And it can be calculated from Eq. (3): [25]
$$
L Y=10^{6}\frac{\mathrm{S}_{e-h}\phi}{\beta E_{g}}(\mathrm{inphotos~MeV}^{-1})
$$
where $\beta$ is a constant parameter with a typical value of about 2.5.
Response time. It is the time that a scintillator converts Xray photons to UV/Vis photons, which is determined by the decay time $(T)$ of the scintillation process. $T$ can be defined as the $1/e$ of the emission intensity, which can be obtained
from Eq. (4) [25]:
$$
\frac{I_{e t}}{I_{e0}}=\mathrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{T}}
$$
where $I_{e0}$ and $I_{e t}$ are the initial emission intensity and the intensity at time $t$ . With a fast response time (short T), a scintillator can be better applied to computerized tomography to obtain real-time imaging or particle detection in accelerators [28].
In addition, several metrics, such as energy resolution, which is defined as the percentage of the full-width-at-halfmaximum (FWHM) value dividing photopeak at specific energy; self-absorption of light, which is concerned with the thickness of the scintillator; proportionality, which is the value to judge the linearity of scintillation response with the X-rays; and emission wavelength, are also important for the performance of a scintillator. [28]
## 2.2 Direct-conversion X-ray detection
Usually, the device structure of a direct-conversion X-ray detector can be a diode-type, which consists of an anode, cathode, and active layer. It transfers the incident $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photons to electrical signals (current or voltage) by using the radiation sensing semiconductors, such as $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{a}}-{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{e}}$ , silicon, germanium, and cadmium zinc telluride [38]. By applying an external electrical field, the carriers (holes and electrons) generated by the X-ray photons are collected by the electrodes [39]. Similar to the optical photodetectors, key metrics to judge the performance of X-ray detectors are sensitivity $(S_{p})$ , mobility-lifetime (μτ) product, detection limit $(D L)$ , signal-noise-ratio (SNR), and so on.
Under a given X-ray dose, a high sensitivity for a directconversion X-ray detector is necessary since it is very important for high-quality images with a good contrast [27]. The sensitivity is concerned with the output current under Xray intensity $(I_{o n})$ , current under dark $(I_{d a r k})$ , irritation dose $(D)$ , and detector volume $(V)$ or area $(A)$ . It can be obtained from Eq. (5):
$$
S_{p}={\frac{I_{\mathrm{on}}-I_{\mathrm{dark}}}{D V}}\mathrm{or}{\frac{I_{\mathrm{on}}-I_{\mathrm{dark}}}{D S}}
$$
Mobility-lifetime $(\mu\tau)$ product demonstrates the drift distance of carriers per unit electric field before annihilation. It reflects the transport characteristics of the material, which is an important parameter to determine the $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray detection sensitivity. It can be obtained in Eq. (6) [40,41]:
$$
I=\frac{I_{0}\mu\tau U}{L^{2}}\frac{1-\exp\Bigl(-\frac{L^{2}}{\mu\tau}\Bigr)}{1+\frac{L s}{U\mu}}
$$
where $I$ is the measured output current, $I_{0}$ is the saturated current, $L$ is the thickness of the active layer, $U$ is the bias voltage, and $s$ is the surface recombination velocity. According to this equation, high $\mu\tau{U}/{L}^{2}$ value results in high output current, indicating $\mu\tau$ product determines the carrier collection efficiency under a certain electric field.
The detection limit is defined as the smallest signal that can be effectively identified, which is determined by the generated signal to noise value. Usually, this signal-noise ratio should be larger than 3. For a direct-conversion X-ray detector, it means the output current produced by an $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray irritation should be three times higher than the dark current. While for a scintillator, it indicates that the emission intensity should be three times higher than the noise intensity [30]. The different application usually requires different detection limit, and the detection limit for a medical diagnostic X-ray detector should below $5.5~{\upmu\mathrm{Gy~s}}^{-1}$ [42].
## 3 Synthesis of MOFs for X-ray detection
Since Yaghi et al. [43] synthesized MOFs using the solvothermal approach in 1995, MOFs with diverse structures and promising applications have generated tremendous academic interest in the last two decades. The solvothermal synthesis is a method through the reaction of original mixture in closed vessels under autogenous pressure above the boiling point of the organic solvent, which has been used as a typical synthetic technique in the construction of a large variety of MOF families such as PCNs, MIL series, and UiOs [44]. Till now, most MOF scintillators are prepared based on this method, and the reaction temperature has played a significant role in the growth of MOFs. For example, Wang et al. [18] synthesized the $\mathrm{M}_{6}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ (carboxylate)12 1 $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Hf}$ or $Z\mathrm{r}$ ) by this method, where the reaction solvent is dimethylformamide (DMF) and the reaction temperature is $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; Lu et al. [45] synthesized a serious of lead(II)-based MOFs by solvothermal method, and the crystal structure of the materials was changed after tuning the solvent type and proportion. However, this method remains a huge challenge that the application for large-scale MOF production is not feasible as MOF synthesis relies on the nucleation at a reactor vessel surface and the production costs are too high [46]. Besides, the crystal size of the MOFs can not be controlled by this method, which is not conveniently applied in X-ray detection. To overcome these drawbacks, Perego, Li, and Liang et al. [31,47,48] synthesized nanoscale MOF scintillators and semiconducting MOFs using modulated solvothermal methods. The particle size was well controlled in several tens nanometers, which can be integrated into polymer matrix to form uniform flexible scintillator films or flexible semiconducting films for X-ray detection.
Non-solvothermal reaction that takes place below or at the boiling point under ambient pressure is a mild, simple, and low-cost MOF synthesis method. Recently, our group has synthesized a series of photochromic and photocontrolled luminescent MOFs using a low-temperature route [49–51]. We prepared three MOFs $\mathrm{([Ln(BCEbpy)(H_{2}O)_{4}][C o^{I I I}}.$ $(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{n}$ , which were assembled from the cationic coordination layers of a viologen functionalized tecton $\mathrm{Ln}(\mathsf{N O})_{3}$ and anionic ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{6}^{3-}$ . These crystalline materials showed reversible photochromic performance and exhibited a photomodulated fluorescence behaviour [51]. Based on this work, we further synthesized low-power $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray-induced photochromic MOFs [52], indicating this simple and mild synthesis method has the potential to prepare high-efficiency MOFs for X-ray detection.
## 4 MOFs X-ray detection
In this section, we introduce the recent progress of MOF scintillators and direct-conversion $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray detectors. Among them, the chemical structure of organic ligands can particularly influence the performance of MOFs. Thus we list the reported organic ligands used in MOFs to realize X-ray detection. As shown in Scheme 1a, by analyzing these organic ligands in detail, we found that most reports used organic scintillator cores [53]. For example, anthracene as core derivative is one of the most popular luminescent organic bridging ligands for MOF scintillators due to their commercial availability, high photoluminescence quantum yield, and fast sensitization [18,31,54]. Besides, naphthalene as a core derivative is also widely used to fabricate MOF scintillators, which showed excellent light intensity as compared with single organic linkers or metal clusters [45,55]. It indicates that organic ligands with radioluminescence properties may be one of the conditions for constructing MOF scintillators.
While for direct-conversion $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray detector, the semiconducting property and sensitive X-ray light absorption are required, and thus the semiquinoid ligand, such as $(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-})$
