[47] and 2,5-diaminobenzene-1,4-dithiol (DABDT) [47], was used as the conducting medium (Scheme 1b).
## 4.1 X-ray scintillation via MOFs
There are two types of luminescence mechanisms reported for MOFs-based X-ray scintillation: (1) the carriers were generated by heavy metal clusters in MOFs and then excited organic luminescent linkers to produce UV/Vis light, which is a typical MLCT mechanism; (2) the electrons are produced by metal-center and absorbed by the organic bridge, and then ligand-to-metal transfer occurs and the characteristic luminescence peaks of metals emit, which is an LMCT mechanism [56]. Before the first reported MOF scintillators, Doty et al. [32,57] showed two kinds of ultra-high energy $(3{\mathrm{~MeV}})$ ion beam-induced luminescence (IBIL) MOFs based on stilbene dicarboxylic acid as an organic luminescence linker and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ ion as the metal cluster center. By changing the reaction solvent from $N{,}N{\ }$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) to $N{,}N{}$ -diethylformamide (DEF), the synthesized MOFs were changed from a two-dimensional (2D) structure to a three-dimensional (3D) one (Figure 3). As compared with commercial organic scintillators such as anthracene single crystal, they not only showed comparable scintillation quantum yield but also had high radiation resistance stability, which aroused the research interest in MOF scintillators.
## 4.1.1 MOF scintillators based on luminescent organic linkers
In 2014, X-ray-excited luminescence of MOFs was first reported by Wang et al. [18]. The materials were formed with high-Z element Hf or $Z\mathrm{r}$ as the metal center (Hf-MOF or $Z\mathrm{r}$ - MOF) and anthracene core derivative 9,10-anthracenyl bis (benzoic acid) $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{L})$ as the bridging ligand, which shows the UiO framework structure as the same as a $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF built from the amino-terphenyldicarboxylate ligand (Figure 4a, b) [58]. Both Hf-MOF and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF showed the radioluminescence (RL) intensity more than ten times higher than $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ alone or physical mixture of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{L}}{+}{\mathrm{MO}}_{2}$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{H}\mathbf{f}$ or Zr) raw materials (Figure 4c). Furthermore, Hf-MOF has stronger luminescence intensity than $Z\mathrm{r-MOF}$ at the same Xray dose because of the higher X-ray absorption coefficient. Both of the MOFs showed a short lifetime of 6.19 (Hf-MOF) and 5.96 ns (Zr-MOF), respectively, which has a similar advantage as compared with traditional organic single crystal and plastic scintillators [53,59,60].
Using the same organic linker and a $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based metal cluster $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}\right)$ , Perego et al. [31] synthesized novel fluorescent MOF nanocrystals (Figure 4d). When embedding the nanocrystal MOFs into the elastomer polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) (Figure 4e), the flexible nanocomposite scintillators showed an ultra-fast scintillation rise time of about 50 ps (inset in Figure 4f) and an average decay time of 4.1 ns (Figure 4f), which has the great potential to be applied in high-resolution X-ray imaging. Recently, Wang et al. [54] reported a one-dimensional lead(II)-based MOF scintillator using another anthracene core derivative $(2E,2^{\prime}E)\ –3,3^{\prime}$ -(anthracene-9,10-diyl) diacrylic acid (H2adda) as the organic luminescence linker, whose chemical structure is shown in Figure $4\mathrm{g}$ . Due to the robust architecture and intrinsic synergetic effect, the material showed a high X-ray sensitivity and a radiation resistance stability (Figure 4h). The fabricated flexible MOF-based scintillator film was firstly applied for $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray imaging, which obtains a spatial resolution of 5.5 lp $\mathrm{mm}^{-1}$ (Figure 4i).
Except for the anthracene core derivatives, naphthalene core derivatives were also used as the organic luminescence linkers [45,55]. Using 1,4-naphthalene dicarboxylate acid $(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ndc})$ , 1,6-naphthalene dicarboxylate acid (1,6- $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ndc})$ , and $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{2}$ as the raw materials, and $\mathrm{DMF/H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{DMA}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the solvents, four kinds of MOF scintillators (SMOF-1, 2, 3, and 4) were synthesized by solvothermal method (Figure 5a). As indicated in Figure 5b–e, the SMOF4 showed a closer packing and solvent-free crystal structure as compared with the other three MOFs, which results in higher radioluminescence intensity [31]. It indicates that lower porosity in MOFs may result in better scintillation performance. After that, the same group reported a series of Pb-based and Ba-based MOF scintillators using naphthalene core derivatives as the organic linkers, which greatly enriched the materials and structures of MOF scintillators [55,61].
## 4.1.2 MOF scintillators based on luminescent metal center
For MOF scintillators, metal clusters can not only serve as Xray absorbing antennas but also act as luminescent centers to achieve X-ray excited luminescence. In 2018, Wang et al.
[33] reported a uranyl-organic framework (SCU-9) that showed X-ray-excited luminescence, and the structure can be seen in Figure 6a. Figure 6b showed the spectra of the SCU-9 under the UV and X-ray excitations, and the well-defined vibrationally-coupled charge-transfer feature for uranyl luminescence indicates that the scintillation mechanism originated from ligand-to-metal charge transfer. Because of the high oxidation state and high atomic number in $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{U}}^{\mathrm{VI}}$ -based MOF, the material showed high X-ray attenuation efficiency, high radiation resistance, and high humidity stability as compared to CsI:Tl, as shown in Figure 6c. Such a good radiation stability for SCU-9 may mainly be attributed to the well-defined coordination bonds between metal clusters and organic ligands. And it can be further improved by designing 3D frameworks, which have more covalent bonds than 2D MOFs. Soon afterward, several kinds of lanthanide-based MOFs (lanthanide $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Eu, Tb) were reported (Figure 6d). It is interesting that Eu-based MOF and Tb-based MOFs emitted characteristic luminescence peaks of Eu and Tb elements (Figure 6e, f). By tuning the doping ratio of $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ in the MOFs, the color of the materials can be tuned from green to red, which provides novel color tuneable scintillators with specific applications [56]. Furthermore, Neufeld et al. [34] synthesized nanoscale MOFs (mean particle size: $162.7\mathrm{nm}$ , Figure 6g–h) constituted by 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid and lanthanide-based (lanthanide $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=\mathrm{E}\mathbf{u}$ , Tb) metal, which also showed excellent X-ray-excited luminescence with specific metal luminescence (Figure 6i). Their low in vitro toxicity suggested that they may be promising materials for in vivo translation.
## 4.2 Direct-conversion X-ray detection based on MOF semiconductors
In addition to realizing X-ray-excited luminescence, the research of direct-conversion X-ray detectors based on MOF semiconductors has attracted great interest. In 2019, Wang et al. [19] reported the first direct radiation detection based on a terbium-based semiconducting MOF $\mathrm{[(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}]T b_{2}L_{3}}.$ $\mathrm{(DMF)}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{HCOO})$ (SCU-12, $\mathrm{L}=\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-})]$ , and the crystal structure can be seen in Figure 7a. As shown in Figure 7b, direct-conversion X-ray detector was fabricated with a photoelectric diode structure. Among them, the pelleted SCU-12 polycrystalline was used as the active layer and Au was used as the cathode/anode. Owing to the high attenuation efficiency provided by heavy metal Tb and the suitable bandgap of the MOF $(1.84\mathrm{eV})$ , the fabricated devices showed a resistivity of $1.6\times10^{11}\Omega$ cm under X-ray radiation (Figure 7c). Besides, it has a high $\mu\tau$ product of $1.3{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}$ , promising $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray sensitivity of $23.8\upmu\mathrm{C}$ ${\mathrm{Gy}_{\mathrm{air}}}^{-1}{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}$ under $80\ensuremath{\mathrm{~kV}}_{\ensuremath{\mathrm{{p}}}}$ , and a very low X-ray detection limit of $0.705~\upmu\mathrm{Gy}\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ (Figure 7d and inset), which has the similar device performance as compared with that of commercially available $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{a}}$ -Se X-ray detector [62].
Subsequently, the same research group reported a threedimensional (3D) polycatenation actinide-based metal-organic-cage (MOC) with semiconducting properties and further used it for direct X-ray detection [63]. As shown in Figure 7e, the MOC was assembled from four hexagonalbipyramidal-coordinated uranyl ions $(\mathrm{UO}_{2}^{2+})$ and six bidentate flexible ligands, which determined a formula of $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}\right)_{4}[(\mathrm{UO}_{2})_{4}(\mathrm{L}_{2})_{6}]$ (altretamine) $_{3}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (SCU-14, $\mathrm{L}_{2}\ =$ $(\mathrm{SC}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{COO})_{2}^{2-})$ . Furthermore, each isolated cage is further interlocked with six adjacent ones through mechanical bonds, which builds a 3D polycatenated MOC. Owing to the strong interlocking mechanical bonds, the material shows a long range $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking throughout the structure, which forms a carrier transmission channel. Besides, the use of heavy element uranium makes SCU-14 present an excellent attenuation efficiency as compared with Tb-based MOF SCU-12 [19] and other commercial materials (Figure 7f). By fabricating the direct-conversion $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diode using this SCU-14 as the active layer and Ag as the electrodes, the device showed a high X-ray sensitivity of $54.93~\upmu\mathrm{C}~\mathrm{Gy_{air}}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Figure $\mathrm{7g}\mathrm{)}$ . Besides, the $\mu\tau$ product of this material reached $6.30{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}$ (Figure $\mathrm{7h}^{\mathrm{-}}$ ), which was higher than that of the reported SCU-12 $\left(1.3{\times}10^{-4}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\thinspace\mathrm{V}^{-1}\right.$ , Figure 7d) and other newly developed perovskite materials [64–66].
Except for the electron transfer between the two organic molecular objects in semiconducting MOFs, the frameworkguest interactions also regulate the exciton behaviors. As shown in Figure 8a, using an electron-acceptor molecule of rhodamine B (RhB) as the guest, a semiconducting MOF of $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}6\mathrm{D}\mathrm{MF}$ (TbTATAB, ${\mathrm{L}}{=}{\mathrm{TATAB}}^{3-}$ , $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}{-}s{-}$ triazine-1,3,5-triyltri- $p$ -aminobenzoate, ${\mathrm{DMF}}{=}N{,}N{\mathrm{.}}$ -dimethylformamide) as the host, the synthesized $\operatorname{RhB}^{+}(\varpi)$ TbTATAB regulated the HOMO/LUMO distribution and the efficient energy transfer up to $77.4\%$ , which improves the conductivity and photoelectric performance of the materials [67]. The fabricated $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray detectors based on $\operatorname{RhB}^{+}(\varpi)$ TbTATAB showed an on-off ratio of 79.5 and high $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray sensitivity of $51.9\upmu\mathrm{C}\mathrm{Gy}_{\mathrm{air}}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is more than 60 and 40 times higher than those of TbTATAB-based devices, respectively (Figure 8b, c). Besides, the $\mu\tau$ product boosted from $3.21\times{10}^{-4}$ to $1.12{\times}10^{-3}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}$ after $\mathrm{RhB}^{+}$ incorporation (Figure 8d). Recently, Han et al. [68] reported a novel MOF guest-host material $\{\mathrm{(EV)}[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\bullet}3.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}$ ( $\mathrm{EV}=$ $N\mathrm{,}N^{\prime}$ -diethyl $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridinium and $\mathbf{ox}\ =$ oxalate), which showed repeatable photochromism and photoelectric conversion under X-ray radiation. As shown in Figure 8e, the use of $\mathrm{EV}^{2+}$ plays as a cation guest and forms cation- $\scriptstyle\cdot\pi$ interactions with the ox ligand, which strongly improves the electron transfer process under $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray irradiation. Thus, it results in a rapid color change (below 30 s) from colorless to blue under Cu-Kα X-rays (Figure 8f) and a large photocurrent response upon irradiation of tungsten anode X-rays. The fabricated $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray detectors showed an ultrahigh X-ray sensitivity of 3216 μC Gyair−1 cm−2 under the bias of 30 V and a μτ product of ${8.27{\times}10}^{-6}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}$ (Figure 8g, h), which is 160 times higher than that of $\mathfrak{a}$ -Se X-ray detector $(\sim20\upmu\mathrm{C}$ ${\mathrm{Gy}_{\mathrm{air}}}^{-1}~{\mathrm{cm}^{-2}})$ , and it provides a new path to improve the performance of X-ray detection using MOF-based materials.
Traditionally, the flat-panel direct-conversion X-ray detectors were fabricated based on inorganic semiconductor single crystals, which has the disadvantage of image overlap in X-ray imaging [38,69]. In recent years, researchers have constructed a high-resolution three-dimensional $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray imaging technology using flexible X-ray detectors, which can greatly improve its resolution [70]. Interestingly, flexible direct-conversion X-ray detectors can be fabricated based on MOFs, such as synthesizing semiconducting nanoscale MOF or hybridizing the semiconducting MOF into the flexible polymer as the active layer [47,48]. In 2020, Liang et al. reported the first MOF-based flexible direct-conversion Xray detector by dispersing micron-sized semiconducting MOF $[(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}]_{2}\mathrm{PbL}_{2}$ $\bar{(\mathrm{L}{=}}\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-},$ ), SCU-13] (Figure 9a) into poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF) as the active layer and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ as the electrodes. As compared with the pelleted device, this MOF-PVDF mixed matrix membrane detector showed higher $\mu\tau$ product and detection sensitivity, increasing from $\bar{3}.76{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}$ and $4.54~\upmu\mathrm{C}~\mathrm{Gy_{air}}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ to $4.{\overset{\cdot}{3}}1{\times}10^{-4}\:\mathrm{cm}^{2}\:\mathrm{V}^{-1}$ and $10.53~{\upmu\mathrm{C~Gy_{\mathrm{air}}}}^{-1}~{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}~\textcircled{\omega}~50~\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 9b). Furthermore, the highest sensitivity of this flexible device can reach $65.86\ \mathrm{\upmuC}\mathrm{~Gy_{air}^{-1}~c m^{-2}}$ at bais $\:=\:100\:\mathrm{V}.\:$ Besides, this device has excellent flexible stability, which showed little photocurrent degradation after 500 bending cycles (Figure 9c). Subsequently, Li et al. [47] designed and synthesized a novel Ni-based semiconducting nanoscale MOF (Ni-DABDT, DABDT $\cdot=2,5$ -diamino-1,4-benzenedithiol dihydrochloride) to fabricate a direct-conversion Xray detector array (Figure 9d). As shown in Figure 9e, this 1D conjugated framework provides $\pi$ -d coupling interaction, which benefits to carrier transport throughout the structure. By fabricating a simple sandwich $\mathrm{\Au/Ni}$ -DABDT/Au photodiode, the devices showed a high sensitivity of $98.6~\upmu\mathrm{C}$ ${\mathrm{Gy}_{\mathrm{air}}}^{-1}{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}$ and a low detection limit of $7.2~\upmu\mathrm{Gy}_{\mathrm{air}}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ under the driving voltage of $1\mathrm{V}.$ . The $\mu\tau$ product was calculated to be $3.26{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}$ , which is much higher than that of the commercial available $_{\mathrm{a-Se}}$ $(1{\times}10^{-6}\mathrm{cm}^{\bar{2}}\mathrm{V}^{-1})[7\mathrm{]}$ 1]. Furthermore, they had excellent radiation and mechanical stability (Figure 9f), which demonstrates great potential in the application of X-ray detection and imaging.
The performance of the reported direct-conversion X-ray detectors was summarized in Table 1. As compared with electron transfer in the frameworks, we can easily find that the framework-guest interactions could strongly improve the X-ray detection performance of these devices. This is because the existence of guest molecules in the cavity of the MOFs can greatly reduce the carrier transport distance between different nodes, resulting in higher $\mu\tau$ product and higher $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray detection sensitivity [67,68]. Accordingly, semiconducting MOFs with a framework-guest structure may be an effective material design strategy for further development of high-sensitivity direct-conversion MOF X-ray detectors.
## 5 Conclusions and perspectives
Radioluminescent and semiconducting MOFs have been increasingly becoming a class of promising materials for Xray detection. As compared with conventional inorganic scintillators, MOF scintillators have relatively mild synthesis conditions and excellent X-ray excited emission wavelength adjustability [53]. Organic ligands-structured MOFs take the advantages of fast decay time, diverse chemical structure, high light yield, high absorption coefficient, and lower toxicity [30,72,73]. Nevertheless, the radiation detection performance of the reported MOF scintillators may not meet the requirements of practical applications, and thus it is of great significance to further improve the RL intensity or light yield of MOF materials. According to Eq. (3), the LY of the scintillators is positive relative to the efficiency of the luminescence quantum yield $(\phi)$ and electron-hole pairs migrating to luminescence center $(S_{e-h})$ . Therefore, introducing organic ligands with high $\phi$ as well as improving the charge transfer efficiency between metal clusters and organic ligands may achieve high RL intensity in MOF scintillators [74]. Besides, the reported MOF scintillators showed that improving the effective atomic number of MOF materials, especially using some heavy rare earth metal clusters with metal center emission capability, is also an important path to obtain high-performance MOF scintillators.
Furthermore, MOF scintillators are usually synthesized by the solvothermal method, while the fabrication of highquality scintillator films is challenging [54]. By dispersing nanocrystals into organic or aqueous solutions and fabricating by the methods of spin coating, scraping coating or spraying, high uniformity films based on nanocrystal MOFs can be obtained. Accordingly, synthesizing nanocrystal
MOFs with recyclability, large-scale production, and low price is meaningful for its application in X-ray detection. And some unconventional synthetic methods, such as mechanochemical synthesis, and microwave-assisted synthesis [46], are the good choice to achieve this purpose. Meanwhile, nanoscale MOFs can be used for X-ray dynamic therapy to treat deep-seated tumors [75]. By synthesizing novel non bio-toxicity nanoscale MOFs with X-ray-excited photodynamic chemical reaction ability ${}^{\cdot1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or ·OH generation), several kinds of cancer cells can be killed in vitro and in vivo [76,77].
Semiconducting MOFs-based direct X-ray detection has potential applications in X-ray imaging by taking the advantages of high X-ray sensitivity, low detection limit, and excellent X-ray irritation stability. The active layer of polycrystalline MOF tablet and nanoscale MOF thin film can be prepared by lamination method and drip coating, respectively [19,47,78]. Since nanoscale patterning is necessary for demonstrating MOFs in miniaturized solid-state devices, several MOF patterning techniques are developed, such as laser induced synthesis [79], photolithography [80], and Xray lithography [81], which provides a great foundation for high precision devices of MOF materials.
In addition, future research of MOF materials should not only focus on the X-ray detection performance, but also design and synthesis of environmentally benign or nonbiotoxicity MOFs. For example, lead-free MOFs or chromium-free MOFs may evolve a new class of X-ray sensors with high performance. By optimizing the X-ray detection efficiency, stability, and greenness, MOFs will have the chance to be really applied in X-ray imaging and biomedical area.
